<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Learning from Mistakes – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning from Mistakes</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Next Time, I Will Study Harder to Avoid...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Speaking: Productive: Explain daily habits and routines. Interactive: Respond to classmates' presentations.</p>
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Speaking: Can explain study habits and routines that support academic growth by sharing specific examples (e.g., "Every morning, I review my vocabulary, and in the evening, I write a summary of what I learned.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can respond to classmates' presentations on learning from mistakes by asking and responding to questions, meeting the specific standard (e.g., "What did you learn from this experience?" "I learned to study harder.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can ask and answer questions about helpful routines and habits in discussions, directly following the specific standard (e.g., "What helps you focus?" "Studying in a quiet room.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b>Warm-up:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher displays 3-4 simple images (e.g., a student looking confused during an exam, a student with a low grade on a report card, a student sleeping during study time).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are asked to identify what they see and orally share what "mistake" they think the student might have made in each image. (E.g., "I see a student looking at bad grades. Maybe they didn't study.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then asks students (using the past simple): "Have you ever made a study mistake like these?" and encourages short oral responses, building on familiar vocabulary like "homework," "grades," "study."</li>
                </ul>
                <b>Modeling:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher models a short example of reflecting on a mistake and setting a future goal, using target grammar and vocabulary. "Last semester, I got a low grade in math because I didn't practice my homework carefully. I learned that practice is important. Next time, I will try to practice every day, and I should ask the teacher for feedback if I don't understand."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of past tense verbs, focusing on /d/ in "improved," /ɪd/ in "wanted," and /t/ in "practiced."</li>
                </ul>
                <b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I should study harder,' am I talking about something I did yesterday or something I need to do for the future?" (Expected answer: For the future / Advice)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If someone says, 'I improved my grades,' are their grades better or worse now?" (Expected answer: Better)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning from Mistakes")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue between two students (Maria and Juan) discussing a recent challenging test, a mistake made, and their plans for improvement. The dialogue will include past simple, future tense, and "should" for advice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner expresses and understands oral messages in different situations, reflecting on mistakes and proposing solutions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (oral):</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students listen and orally answer: "What problem did Maria have?" "What advice did Juan give her?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed questions (oral):</b> The teacher plays the audio again. Students orally respond to: "What mistake did Maria learn from?" (e.g., "She learned she didn't study well.") "What will she do next time?" (e.g., "She will review her vocabulary and practice more.") "What study habits did Juan mention are successful?" (e.g., "Studying in a quiet room and asking for feedback.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher emphasizes key vocabulary (e.g., "challenging," "feedback," "progress," "improvement") and pronunciation of past tense verbs heard in the dialogue.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using past simple for experiences, future tense for improvements, and "should" for advice, along with target vocabulary, focusing on clear pronunciation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Oral Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides sentence beginnings and students complete them orally with appropriate past simple verbs, future tense, or "should," using target vocabulary. E.g., Teacher: "Last week, I felt that my homework was very..." Students: "...challenging." Teacher: "I got a bad grade, so next time, I will..." Students: "...study more carefully." Teacher: "To improve your English, you should..." Students: "...practice speaking every day." The teacher corrects pronunciation of past tense verbs ending in -ed as needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Advice Chain Drill (Semi-controlled Practice):</b> Students sit in a circle. The first student shares a simple past mistake: "Last month, I didn't get good grades because I didn't study." The next student gives advice using "should" and "will": "You should study better. Next time, you will improve your grades." Then they share their own mistake. This continues around the circle. The teacher models clear pronunciation, especially for minimal pairs (e.g., distinguishing /ð/ in "they" from /d/ in "day" when these words arise naturally in student speech, or /θ/ in "think" from /s" in "sink").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interview: Habits for Success (Pair Interaction):</b> Students work in pairs. They interview each other using questions related to study habits and routines, incorporating "What helps you focus?" "How do you improve your grades?" "What daily habits support your progress?" They respond using examples and target vocabulary. (e.g., "Studying in a quiet room helps me focus." "Every morning, I review my vocabulary." "I try to learn new words carefully.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning from Mistakes")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group "My Learning Story" Presentation and Constructive Feedback.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups of 3-4. Each student prepares a short oral presentation (approx. 1 minute) about a specific academic mistake or challenge they faced in the past (using past simple).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In their presentation, they must explain what they "learned from this experience" (Pragmatic: Reflecting on learning) and clearly state what they "will do differently next time" to achieve "improvement" and "success" (using future tense and setting goals for improvement). They should also mention helpful daily "habits" or "routines" (e.g., "Every evening, I will review my notes carefully.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After each presentation, the other group members will listen actively and provide "constructive feedback" and advice using "should" (Sociolinguistic: Provide constructive feedback). They will also ask 1-2 follow-up questions to their classmates (e.g., "What did you learn specifically from this mistake?" "You should try to practice more with a partner. What helps you focus when you study?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, listens, and takes notes on student fluency, accuracy, and interaction, providing minimal support to encourage autonomous communication.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "My Learning Story" presentations and participate in the group feedback session from Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Fluency:</b> Explains habits/routines with good flow.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Accurately uses Past Simple, Future Tense, and "should."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Incorporates target vocabulary (mistakes, grades, study, habits, feedback, improve, better, carefully, etc.).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Generally intelligible, with effort on past tense -ed endings and clear word distinctions (e.g., minimal pairs).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interaction:</b> Asks and answers questions, provides constructive feedback.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Completion:</b> Successfully explains a mistake, what was learned, and a plan for improvement.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Oral reflection):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new habit you learned about today that you will try to include in your study routine?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you find challenging about explaining your learning story, and how will you improve next time?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now explaining your learning from mistakes and giving advice to others?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific vocabulary or grammar points from today's lesson will help you speak better about your studies?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., The chain drill got students speaking quickly; the group presentations fostered good interaction).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson or future speaking activities? (e.g., Provide more sentence starters for feedback; explicitly review minimal pairs related to words like 'fail' vs 'whale' if pronunciation challenges persist).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking lesson prepared students to orally articulate their experiences and plans. In Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), students will transition to writing a short paragraph about a past mistake, what they learned, and their plan for improvement, solidifying the use of past simple, future tense, and "should" in written form.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>